<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/expert/demography">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the expert tier of Sociology, and it closes the unit's family questions with the
    discipline that counts them: demography. It builds on the intermediate lessons <em>Society
    and Socialisation</em> — whose theorists return here as the functionalist account of the
    family — and <em>Gender and Society</em>, whose care economy and stalled revolution are the
    human mechanics of falling fertility, with <em>Class and Stratification</em> supplying the
    dependency arithmetic. The advanced <em>Globalisation and Society</em> returns later in the
    lesson as the migration wildcard.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>walk the four stages of the demographic transition and name the causal drivers behind each fall in mortality and fertility;</li>
    <li>do the household arithmetic that turns changing family forms into housing, energy and pension demand;</li>
    <li>read an age pyramid and say what its shape implies about growth, ageing and policy decades ahead;</li>
    <li>explain population momentum — why a country at replacement fertility keeps growing for decades, and why policy acts with a generation's lag;</li>
    <li>weigh the second transition's evidence and state what family policy can and cannot do.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Demography is <strong>the mathematics of populations — births, deaths and migration — and the
    social science with the most reliable forecasts</strong>: everyone who will be 40 in 2060 is
    already born. Its master pattern, the demographic transition, explains the population boom,
    the ageing of nations and the century's biggest open question. And its human subject is the
    family: the household is where births are decided, care is organised and generations
    co-reside, so every demographic trend is at bottom a story about how family forms are
    changing. This chapter reads the unit's three family questions — types, roles, and change —
    through the demographic machinery that measures them.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the transition, momentum, age structures, the second transition" widget="equationStep" config={demogCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> press "Walk me through it" and the four steps appear one at
    a time — try predicting each note before revealing it, and use the reset button to rehearse
    the sequence from memory. Watch the chain the steps build: the transition is about
    <em>rates</em>, momentum about <em>age structure</em>, age structures about <em>politics</em>,
    and the second transition about the <em>shape of family life</em>. Each step converts a
    demographic fact into a social consequence — the lesson's whole argument in miniature.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the different types of families — and how do we count them?</h2>
  <p>
    The census categories are demographic instruments: nuclear families (couple with children),
    extended and multigenerational households, lone-parent families, reconstituted (step) families,
    childless couples and one-person households. The counts have moved dramatically. One-person
    households are the growth story of the rich world — roughly a third of households in much of
    Europe, and nearly two-fifths in Japan (where single-person households became the largest
    single category in the 2010s) — a statistic invisible in family sociology until demography
    priced it: solo living multiplies housing demand, appliances and energy per head. Average
    household size has fallen almost everywhere (from over four people in mid-century Europe to
    under 2.5 in much of it), driven jointly by fertility decline, divorce and ageing. Lone-parent
    families cluster where divorce and non-marital births are high (roughly a quarter of children
    in the US and UK live with one parent, against under a tenth in much of continental Europe —
    policy and norms, not biology). The typology matters because benefits, pensions and housing
    markets are built on household units; when the units change shape, the systems leak.
  </p>
  <p>
    The arithmetic behind the leak is worth doing once. Suppose a city's population stays at
    one million while average household size falls from 4.0 to 2.4 — both ends of that range
    are historically real. Households rise from 250,000 to roughly 417,000: two-thirds more
    dwellings, meters, boilers and rubbish collections <em>with zero population growth</em>.
    This is why demographers watch household formation as closely as births: housing demand,
    pension eligibility, consumer markets and energy forecasts all key off the household
    unit, and the unit is changing shape faster than the population is changing size. Japan's
    household count growing while its population declines is the standard worked case.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the extended family has disappeared — everyone now lives
    in nuclear households." The proportions shifted, not the forms: multigenerational living
    persists or grows across much of Asia, Africa and southern Europe, and has been rising in
    the US for decades as young adults return to the parental home in record numbers. What
    vanished is the <em>default</em>, not the family type.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the nuclear family is the historical norm and everything else
    is modern decay." Historically, extended and complex households dominated much of the world,
    nuclear dominance in the West is largely a twentieth-century phenomenon, and one-person
    households existed in early-modern cities too. The family forms are old; their statistical
    proportions are what move.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the census categories are measuring instruments, and the arithmetic
    is unforgiving — the same million people at 2.4 per household need roughly two-thirds more
    dwellings than at 4.0. Next we ask what the family is actually <em>for</em>, and how
    demography converts each of its functions into numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>What is the family for — individual function and social arithmetic?</h2>
  <p>
    Functionally, the family is society's first institution: reproduction (the demographic
    input), socialisation (primary, as the previous chapter's theorists formalised), economic
    cooperation (the historical production unit — the family farm, the shop above the flat) and
    welfare (childcare, eldercare, insurance between generations). Demography translates each
    function into arithmetic. Reproduction: fertility is now largely a chosen variable, which
    makes the family the site where the transition actually happens — women's education and
    employment are the strongest predictors of falling fertility everywhere. Welfare: the
    family is the largest unpaid care provider on earth, and its capacity is shrinking precisely
    as demand rises — the old-age dependency ratio (workers per retiree) roughly halves in most
    rich countries by 2050, and the informal care it implies cannot be legislated into existence.
    The starkest case is China's "4-2-1" structure: one child potentially responsible for two
    parents and four grandparents, a demographic debt booked by the one-child policy (1979–2015)
    and now inherited. The family's functions did not disappear with modernisation; they
    migrated — partly to the state (pensions, schools), partly to the market (childcare), and
    the migration is incomplete everywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    The family-sociology tradition named the same functions from the other direction. Murdock
    (1949) listed four universal family functions — sexual, reproductive, economic and
    educational — and Parsons made the claim historical: the industrial nuclear family exists
    because a geographically mobile workforce needs small, portable units, with the family
    "specialising" in personality stabilisation while the factory took over production. The
    critics sharpened the model rather than demolishing it: feminist sociology (Oakley's
    <em>The Sociology of Housework</em>, 1974) showed the functions rest on unpaid and
    unequally distributed domestic labour; Young and Willmott's <em>The Symmetrical Family</em>
    (1973) tracked conjugal roles converging as wives entered paid work; and the personal-life
    perspective (Smart) warned that functions listed from outside miss what families mean to
    their members. The debate is the topic's live form: is the family defined by what it does
    for society, or by what its members do for each other — and can demography even see the
    second question?
  </p>
  <p>
    The classic statement of that arithmetic is the <strong>demographic transition</strong>,
    and it is worth walking its stages. Stage one (pre-transition): birth rates and death
    rates both high — around 35–40 per 1,000 per year — so populations barely grow. Stage
    two: death rates fall (sanitation, vaccines, nutrition) while birth rates stay high —
    the boom; this is where a population can double in a generation. Stage three: birth
    rates fall (urbanisation, education, contraception, the rising cost of children) and
    growth slows. Stage four: low birth and death rates, growth near zero, and the age
    structure skewing old. The timing mismatch — mortality falls in years, fertility in
    decades — is what created the twentieth century's population explosion, and each
    country's position on the sequence is legible in its population pyramid today.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the demographic transition is a natural law — every country
    passes through the same stages automatically." It is a pattern with causes, not a mechanism
    on rails: the stages run on identifiable drivers (sanitation, female education,
    contraceptive access, urban housing costs), countries reach them at different speeds and in
    different orders, and several have stalled or jumped. Treating the transition as automatic
    is what produced the field's classic forecasting errors — projections in both directions,
    some countries' fertility falling far faster than assumed, others stalling above
    expectation — and the honest reading keeps the causal drivers in front of the curve.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the family's functions did not vanish with modernisation — they
    migrated to the state and the market — and the transition's stages run on identifiable
    drivers, not rails. The remaining question is what happens when fertility falls below
    replacement and the shape of family life itself changes.
  </p>

  <h2>How is family life changing? The second transition</h2>
  <p>
    The first demographic transition moved births and deaths from high to low; the second
    (Lesthaeghe and van de Kaa's term) changes the <em>shape</em> of family life: below-
    replacement fertility, delayed marriage and childbearing, rising cohabitation and
    childlessness, divorce plateauing high, and partnership increasingly decoupled from
    reproduction. The numbers are stark: South Korea's total fertility rate hit 0.72 in 2023 —
    the lowest ever recorded, barely a third of replacement (2.1) — while over half the world's
    countries now sit below replacement. Marriage has not vanished; it has become a capstone
    rather than a cornerstone (contracted after careers and cohabitation, later and less
    universally), and first-marriage ages have risen by years in a generation. The
    consequences are demographic, not just sociological: fewer children mean fewer workers
    supporting each retiree, and the UN's 2024 projection — a peak near 10.3 billion in the
    mid-2080s, then decline — depends almost entirely on how fast Africa's family-size
    transition runs. Whether pronatalist policy can bend the curve is the field's live
    experiment: Korea's massive spending has so far produced at best marginal recoveries,
    because the drivers (housing costs, career penalties, gender-unequal domestic work) are
    structural.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "replacement fertility is 2.0 children per woman." It is
    2.1 in low-mortality settings — the extra tenth replaces children who die before
    reproducing — and substantially higher where mortality is high, which is why "replacement"
    is a moving target across development, and why a country can sit at 2.1 and still grow
    through momentum.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pronatal policy, the honest ledger.</strong> Hungary runs the world's most expensive
    attempt to bend the curve: around 5% of GDP in family support (income-tax exemption for
    mothers of four, heavily subsidised loans cancelled per child), against a fertility path
    that rose from 1.23 (2011) to about 1.6 (2021) — and then slipped back toward 1.3 by 2025.
    The rise was real; the attribution is contested, since timing shifts (women advancing births
    they would have had anyway) can lift the period rate for years without changing completed
    family size, and work-family reconciliation models (the Nordic pattern) spend differently
    and measure differently. The lesson demographers draw is sober: policy can remove obstacles
    — childcare, housing, career penalties — but no intervention has yet recreated the family
    systems that once produced replacement fertility on their own.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the four stages of the demographic transition, and state precisely what changes between stage two and stage three.</li>
    <li>A city holds steady at one million people while average household size falls from 4.0 to 2.4. Estimate the change in the number of households, and explain which systems the shift strains.</li>
    <li>Why can a country at replacement fertility (2.1) keep growing for decades — and what does that imply about how quickly family policy can work?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Birth and death rates both high; then death rates fall while
    births stay high (the boom); then birth rates fall — urbanisation, education, contraception,
    the rising cost of children — and growth slows; finally low birth and death rates with the
    age structure skewing old. The stage-two-to-three hinge is <em>fertility</em> finally
    following mortality down, which is why women's education and employment are the strongest
    predictors of it. (2) Households rise from 250,000 to roughly 417,000 — two-thirds more
    dwellings, meters, boilers and rubbish collections with zero population growth. Benefits,
    pensions and housing markets are built on the household unit, so when the unit changes shape
    faster than the population changes size, the systems leak. (3) Momentum: growth is driven by
    age structure, not just the birth rate — the unusually large cohorts already born keep
    entering childbearing, so births exceed deaths even at two children per woman. Policy
    therefore acts on fertility with a generation's lag, which is why China's "4-2-1" care debt
    was booked decades before it came due.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Demography is the mathematics of populations — births, deaths, migration — and the social science with the most reliable forecasts: everyone who will be 40 in 2060 is already born.</li>
    <li>The demographic transition is a pattern with causes — sanitation, female education, contraceptive access, urban housing costs — not a mechanism on rails.</li>
    <li>Household formation moves faster than population size: one million people at 2.4 per household need roughly two-thirds more dwellings than the same million at 4.0.</li>
    <li>The family remains the site where the transition happens; women's education and employment are the strongest predictors of falling fertility everywhere.</li>
    <li>The second transition changes the shape of family life — below-replacement fertility, delayed marriage, rising childlessness — with South Korea's 0.72 (2023) the recorded extreme.</li>
    <li>Population momentum means age structure, not just birth rates, drives growth; demography is destiny at 20-year latency.</li>
    <li>Projections split into the near-certain (the people already born) and the assumption-driven (future fertility) — the UN's published variants mark which is which.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Migration — the wildcard every rich-country projection lists as its widest uncertainty band —
    is an opening theme of the advanced <em>Globalisation and Society</em>. The ties that carry
    jobs, information and norms through the households counted here are the subject of
    <em>Social Networks</em>, and the dependency ratio's pension arithmetic returns in the
    intermediate <em>Class and Stratification</em> as a question about who pays for whom.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The mortality half of the transition, worked.</strong> Global life expectancy sat
    near 30 years through the mid-nineteenth century and runs above 73 today (WHO) — and the
    mechanism was mostly not medicine: the big gains came from clean water, sanitation,
    nutrition and (later) vaccination collapsing infant and child mortality, since a death at
    age 1 costs more expectancy than a death at 70. Omran's epidemiological transition names
    the sequence — pandemics and famine give way to chronic degenerative disease as the
    dominant killer — and its current frontier country is the transition's newest variant:
    societies reaching old age (Nigeria's median age is about 18) before they reach prosperity,
    growing old before growing rich.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Migration, the wildcard.</strong> Births and deaths move slowly and predictably;
    migration can change both direction and pace within a budget cycle. Its arithmetic:
    roughly 280 million people (about 3.6% of humanity) live outside their birth country, and
    remittances to low- and middle-income countries — World Bank estimates put them well over
    600 billion dollars a year — now run at several times official development assistance.
    The demographic irony: ageing societies need young workers, young economies need old
    societies' capital, and the flows that would balance both are the ones politics finds
    hardest to authorise — which is why every rich-country projection lists migration as its
    widest uncertainty band.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Population momentum, worked.</strong> Why does a country at replacement fertility
    keep growing for decades? Because age structure, not just birth rate, drives growth: if the
    childbearing-age cohorts are unusually large (the echo of a past boom), births exceed deaths
    even at two children per woman. China illustrates the reverse: momentum now runs downward —
    its population peaked (2022, per official data) and will shrink for decades even if
    fertility recovers somewhat, because the cohorts entering old age dwarf those entering
    childbearing. Policy acts on fertility with a generation's lag; demography is destiny at
    20-year latency.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The forecast that is actually reliable.</strong> Population projections divide into
    a near-certain part (the people already born — cohort sizes are known) and an assumption-
    driven part (future fertility, mortality, migration). Age pyramids 30 years out are
    engineering-grade; 80-year projections are scenarios with confidence bands. The discipline's
    honesty about which is which is a model for the social sciences: the UN publishes variants
    (high/low/medium), and the medium variant's record over recent decades is genuinely good —
    while its misses (faster fertility decline than expected) are exactly what revised the 2080s
    peak downward between the 2022 and 2024 editions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Will the fertility fall in sub-Saharan Africa follow the
    East Asian speed or the slower historical pace (the largest uncertainty in every global
    projection)? Can ageing societies import youth without political rupture — is migration the
    wildcard that beats arithmetic? And does ultra-low fertility have a floor (the "lowest-low"
    literature asks whether 0.7 is survivable at all)? Each question ties the family topic of
    this unit to the fiscal and geopolitical arguments of the next half-century.
  </p>

